+These calls execute immediately and bypass the change tracker, so check the whole write workflow:
+
+- Loaded entities keep their old values and snapshots. A later change to the same properties through
+  a tracked entity can overwrite the SQL change, even in one sequential request. Use one coherent
+  tracked save, or keep the set-based path free of tracked copies; a transaction alone does not
+  refresh tracked state.
+- Configured concurrency tokens are not checked. When optimistic concurrency is required, keep the
+  token predicate, the token update, and the affected-row check.
+- Each call executes on its own: a later `SaveChanges` neither batches it nor makes it atomic with
+  audit or outbox writes. Use one save unit, or an explicit shared transaction where atomicity is
+  required.
+- Client-side cascades and `SaveChanges`-based hooks do not run; command interceptors still see the
+  SQL. Keep the domain behavior those hooks carry instead of bypassing it to save queries.
